    <fr:title text="Oracle Reductions and Composition">Oracle Reductions and Composition</fr:title>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p><fr:link href="/dhsorens-0040/" title="ArkLib" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0040/" display-uri="dhsorens-0040" type="local">ArkLib</fr:link>'s organising abstraction, and the reason it can hope to cover many protocols rather than one. A reduction maps one relation to another; a protocol is a composition of reductions; completeness and soundness are proved once per component.
</html:p>
    <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The definition: relations with oracle statements, and what a reduction between them is.</html:li>
        <html:li>Sequential composition, and how the soundness error accounts.</html:li>
        <html:li>Lifting and virtual oracle reductions (<html:code>LiftContext</html:code>) — why real protocols need a component to run in a larger context than it was stated in.</html:li>
        <html:li>Decomposing a full system this way. <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003C/" title="WHIR" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003C/" display-uri="dhsorens-003C" type="local">WHIR</fr:link> or <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003G/" title="Spartan and Sumcheck-Based SNARKs" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003G/" display-uri="dhsorens-003G" type="local">Spartan</fr:link> is the test case.</html:li>
        <html:li>What is lost: which protocols resist a clean decomposition, and why.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
    <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib" type="external">ArkLib</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://lean-lang.org/use-cases/arklib/" type="external">ArkLib on lean-lang.org</fr:link>.
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
