            <fr:title text="The Shape of a Proof System">The Shape of a Proof System</fr:title>
            <fr:meta name="toc">true</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    The vocabulary layer. <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0037/" title="FRI" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0037/" display-uri="dhsorens-0037" type="local">FRI</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003G/" title="Spartan and Sumcheck-Based SNARKs" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003G/" display-uri="dhsorens-003G" type="local">Spartan</fr:link>, and <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003C/" title="WHIR" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003C/" display-uri="dhsorens-003C" type="local">WHIR</fr:link> are instances of the same handful of definitions, and much of what is confusing in a paper is a confusion about which definition is in play: which soundness notion, in which oracle model, against which adversary.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Two questions this section exists to answer. What does a security proof for a SNARK actually establish? And at which step of the compilation from interactive protocol to deployed system does the security get lost?
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/dhsorens/" title="Derek Sorensen" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens/" display-uri="dhsorens" type="local">Derek Sorensen</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>8</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>18</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:uri>https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002E/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>dhsorens-002E</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/dhsorens-002E/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Interactive Proofs, Arguments, and IOPs">Interactive Proofs, Arguments, and IOPs</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The chain of definitions that everything else is stated in terms of, ending at the interactive oracle reduction that <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0040/" title="ArkLib" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0040/" display-uri="dhsorens-0040" type="local">ArkLib</fr:link> takes as primitive. <html:em>Proof</html:em> does different work here than it does in <fr:link href="/dhsorens-001F/" title="Proposition and Proof in Mathematics and Computer Science" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-001F/" display-uri="dhsorens-001F" type="local">proposition and proof</fr:link>: a SNARK convinces a verifier with high probability under stated assumptions, and is not a derivation.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Interactive proof, argument (prover computationally bounded), PCP, IOP, oracle reduction — what each relaxation buys.</html:li>
        <html:li>Where <html:em>succinct</html:em> and <html:em>of knowledge</html:em> enter, and what each costs the construction.</html:li>
        <html:li>Public-coin versus private-coin, and why every deployed system is public-coin.</html:li>
        <html:li>The knowledge extractor: what it is handed, what it must produce, and why rewinding is a problem in the non-interactive setting.</html:li>
        <html:li>Zero-knowledge — honest-verifier versus malicious, and how much of it a zkVM actually needs. Mostly these systems are bought for succinctness.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
                <html:p>
    Reading: <html:code>ArkLib/OracleReduction/Basic.lean</html:code> as the definitional reference; Thaler, <html:em>Proofs, Arguments, and Zero-Knowledge</html:em>, for the textbook chain.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
