                <fr:title text="Soundness Notions">Soundness Notions</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    Which soundness a theorem is about determines whether it survives the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002G/" title="Fiat–Shamir and the Random Oracle Model" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002G/" display-uri="dhsorens-002G" type="local">Fiat–Shamir transform</fr:link>. A table of the notions, the implications between them, and which compilation step preserves each belongs on this page.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Soundness, knowledge soundness, special soundness — and the implications between them.</html:li>
        <html:li>Round-by-round soundness and state-restoration soundness: what they demand, and why they were invented.</html:li>
        <html:li>Soundness error versus security level in bits; how error accumulates across rounds, queries, and repetitions.</html:li>
        <html:li>Provable versus conjectured soundness, and which deployed parameters rest on which. See <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002P/" title="List Decoding and Proximity Gaps" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002P/" display-uri="dhsorens-002P" type="local">proximity gaps</fr:link>.</html:li>
        <html:li>What "128-bit security" means for a hash-based system once <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003A/" title="Batching and Grinding" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003A/" display-uri="dhsorens-003A" type="local">grinding</fr:link> is counted.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
                <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/118" type="external">How to Prove False Statements</fr:link> for why the distinctions are not academic.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
