                <fr:title text="Fiat–Shamir and the Random Oracle Model">Fiat–Shamir and the Random Oracle Model</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The step that makes a proof system deployable, and the step where the security argument is thinnest.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The transform, and exactly what it assumes about the hash function.</html:li>
        <html:li>Why <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002F/" title="Soundness Notions" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002F/" display-uri="dhsorens-002F" type="local">round-by-round soundness</fr:link> is the right hypothesis for a many-round protocol.</html:li>
        <html:li>Khovratovich, Rothblum, and Soukhanov's attack: GKR made non-interactive proves false statements. What it does and does not say about deployed provers.</html:li>
        <html:li>Duplex sponges versus hashing a transcript; domain separation and salting, and what ArkLib's <html:code>Salt.lean</html:code> is for.</html:li>
        <html:li>Whether the ROM is the right idealisation, and what the alternatives cost.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
                <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/118" type="external">How to Prove False Statements</fr:link>, <fr:link href="https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib" type="external">ArkLib</fr:link> (<html:code>OracleReduction/FiatShamir</html:code>).
</html:p>
