                <fr:title text="The BCS Transform">The BCS Transform</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    How an IOP plus a commitment scheme becomes a non-interactive argument. The transform that every hash-based system in deployment is an instance of.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The construction: commit to each oracle, answer queries with openings, then <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002G/" title="Fiat–Shamir and the Random Oracle Model" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002G/" display-uri="dhsorens-002G" type="local">Fiat–Shamir</fr:link>.</html:li>
        <html:li>What the commitment must provide — position binding is not obviously enough; where extractability is needed.</html:li>
        <html:li>How the IOP's query complexity turns into proof size, concretely.</html:li>
        <html:li>The security loss in the reduction, and whether it is tight.</html:li>
        <html:li>ArkLib's <html:code>OracleReduction/BCS</html:code>: what is formalised and what the statement looks like.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
                <html:p>
    Sources: Ben-Sasson, Chiesa, and Spooner, <html:em>Interactive Oracle Proofs</html:em>; <fr:link href="https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib" type="external">ArkLib</fr:link>.
</html:p>
