            <fr:title text="Spartan and Sumcheck-Based SNARKs">Spartan and Sumcheck-Based SNARKs</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p><fr:link href="/dhsorens-002S/" title="R1CS" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002S/" display-uri="dhsorens-002S" type="local">R1CS</fr:link> proved with <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002Q/" title="The Sumcheck Protocol" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002Q/" display-uri="dhsorens-002Q" type="local">sumcheck</fr:link> instead of quotients, which removes the FFT and moves the cost into a sparse polynomial commitment. It is also one of the systems <fr:link href="/dhsorens-0040/" title="ArkLib" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-0040/" display-uri="dhsorens-0040" type="local">ArkLib</fr:link> has formalised, so it is a good place to read a full argument in Lean.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The reduction from R1CS satisfiability to a sumcheck claim.</html:li>
        <html:li>Sparse polynomial commitment (Spark) and why the matrices' sparsity is what makes this cheap.</html:li>
        <html:li>Prover cost profile against a Plonkish system on the same circuit.</html:li>
        <html:li>The line from here to <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003N/" title="Beyond Nova: HyperNova, ProtoStar, ProtoGalaxy" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003N/" display-uri="dhsorens-003N" type="local">HyperNova</fr:link>, which folds the same shape of claim.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
