            <fr:title text="WHIR">WHIR</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    The current endpoint of the lineage: a multilinear commitment with a very fast verifier, unifying the Reed–Solomon proximity line with the tensor-commitment line that Basefold began.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>The construction, and where <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002Q/" title="The Sumcheck Protocol" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002Q/" display-uri="dhsorens-002Q" type="local">sumcheck</fr:link> enters the low-degree test.</html:li>
        <html:li>How a multilinear claim is served by a univariate proximity test — the translation, and its cost.</html:li>
        <html:li>The verifier-time claim, and what it is measured against.</html:li>
        <html:li>Which of <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003B/" title="STIR" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003B/" display-uri="dhsorens-003B" type="local">STIR</fr:link>'s ideas it keeps and which it discards.</html:li>
        <html:li>ArkLib's WHIR formalisation: what is proved, and what the remaining obligations are.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
            <html:p>
    Sources: Arnon, Chiesa, Fenzi, and Yogev, <html:em>WHIR</html:em>; <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2026/1367" type="external">SoK: Hash-Based Polynomial Commitments</fr:link>; <fr:link href="https://github.com/Verified-zkEVM/ArkLib" type="external">ArkLib</fr:link>.
</html:p>
